# Ticket & Project Board Management

## GitHub Project Board

The Shareland project board is at: https://github.com/orgs/Shareland/projects/1

Use the `gh` CLI to interact with issues and the project board.

## Creating Issues

When the user describes bugs, features, or tasks conversationally, extract each into a well-structured GitHub Issue.

**IMPORTANT:** Do NOT use labels for type, priority, or size. Use the native Issue Type and project board fields instead.

### Issue Types (native GitHub feature)

Set type via API after creating the issue:

| Type | When to use |
|------|-------------|
| Bug | Something is broken or behaving unexpectedly |
| Feature | New functionality or enhancement |
| Task | Chores, refactoring, infra, documentation |

```powershell
# Set type after creation (gh CLI doesn't support --type yet)
gh api repos/Shareland/<repo>/issues/<number> --method PATCH -f type="Bug"   # or "Feature" or "Task"
```

### Labels

Only use labels for **cross-cutting categories** that aren't covered by Type or project fields:
- `infra` — CI, deployment, DevOps, tooling
- `research` — Investigation, spike, or open question
- `blocked` — Waiting on external dependency or decision
- `documentation` — Documentation-related
- `duplicate` — For closing duplicates

**Do NOT use:** `bug`, `enhancement`, `P0`-`P3`, `good first issue`, `help wanted`, `invalid`, `question`, `wontfix`

### Bug Report Template

Write the body to a temp file, then create the issue:

```powershell
$body = @"
## Bug Description
<What's happening>

## Expected Behavior
<What should happen>

## Steps to Reproduce
1. <step>
2. <step>

## Affected Area
<Component/page/route affected>
"@
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText("$env:TEMP\issue-body.md", $body)
$url = gh issue create --repo Shareland/<repo-name> --title "<concise title>" --body-file "$env:TEMP\issue-body.md"
Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\issue-body.md" -Force
$number = ($url -split '/')[-1]
gh api repos/Shareland/<repo-name>/issues/$number --method PATCH -f type="Bug"
```

### Feature Request Template

```powershell
$body = @"
## Feature Description
<What needs to be built>

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] <Criterion 1>
- [ ] <Criterion 2>

## Affected Repositories
- <Which repos need changes>
"@
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText("$env:TEMP\issue-body.md", $body)
$url = gh issue create --repo Shareland/<repo-name> --title "<concise title>" --body-file "$env:TEMP\issue-body.md"
Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\issue-body.md" -Force
$number = ($url -split '/')[-1]
gh api repos/Shareland/<repo-name>/issues/$number --method PATCH -f type="Feature"
```

### Choosing the Right Repo for Issues
- **UI/UX bugs or features**: `Shareland/shareland-react`
- **API endpoints, Prisma, email, KMS**: `Shareland/shareland-api`
- **Oracle pricing pipeline, statistics daemon, scheduled data processing**: `Shareland/shareland-daemons` (NOT `shareland-api` — the API owns HTTP endpoints and Prisma; the daemons own the pipeline)
- **Smart contract changes**: `Shareland/shareland-contracts`
- **Shared types, market formulas, utilities used by both react and api**: `Shareland/shareland-common`
- **Documentation updates**: `Shareland/shareland-docs` (docs.share.land, Mintlify)
- **Internal KPI dashboards / daily brief**: `Shareland/shareland-mission-control`
- **Agentic prototyping**: `Shareland/shareland-prototypes` — only board it if it is real tracked work; throwaway exploration should not create board noise
- **Infra, tooling, charting, OG scraping**: `shareland-infra`, `dev-tools`, `trading-view-charting`, `open-graph-scraper`
- **Cross-repo features**: Create in the primary repo, mention others in the description

## Ticket Flows (which board columns apply)

Repos ship in three different ways, and **the flow decides which board columns are valid.** Pushing a deploy-on-merge repo through the exchange release pipeline is the most common mistake.

| Flow | Repos | Board path after In Review |
|---|---|---|
| **Exchange stack** | `shareland-react`, `shareland-api`, `shareland-contracts`, `shareland-daemons` | Merged → On Testnet → Live - Verify → Closed |
| **Deploy on merge** | `shareland-docs`, `shareland-mission-control`, `shareland-prototypes` | Live - Verify → Closed |
| **Deploy after merge** | `shareland-common`, `shareland-infra`, `dev-tools`, `open-graph-scraper`, `trading-view-charting` | Merged → Live - Verify → Closed |

**Only the exchange stack has a testnet.** Those four repos are the only ones that can change testnet (Base Sepolia, testnet.share.land), so **`On Testnet` belongs to them and to nothing else.**

- **Exchange stack** — merging does not deploy. A release or a manual deploy does, which makes `Merged` a real and durable state. Skip `On Testnet` only when a change genuinely never touches testnet. PRs are conventional across the stack, but only `shareland-contracts` enforces them through branch protection.
- **Deploy on merge** — the merge *is* the deploy: landing on `main` goes live at once via Vercel or Mintlify. `Merged` would last seconds, so do not use it; go straight to `Live - Verify`. **These three are the only deploy-on-merge repos.** PRs are recommended on `shareland-mission-control` and `shareland-prototypes` to avoid collisions when someone else may be working in the repo; they are not enforced, and direct pushes are fine for solo or trivial changes.
- **Deploy after merge** — merging is not enough, but there is no testnet either. `shareland-common` publishes to the package registry and consumers bump the version; the tooling repos get applied or picked up in use. Park the ticket in `Merged` until that happens, then `Live - Verify`. If a repo ships nothing at all — `shareland-webapp` is kept only for reference — stop at `Merged` and close.

These three flows were previously called Tier A, B and C. If you meet that phrasing in an older doc or PR, it maps in the same order. There is no fourth tier.

### Repo-owned rules win locally

This file is synced to eight repos (listed in `project-overview.mdc`). Some of those repos also define their own `.cursor/rules/`. `shareland-mission-control` is the clearest case: it **closes issues at deploy** instead of after verification. Where a repo's own rule conflicts with this file, **follow the repo's rule** for work in that repo.

## Adding Issues to the Project Board

Every issue must be added to the project board with **Status**, **Priority**, and **Size** set. Use the following IDs:

### Project & Field IDs

```
Project ID:  PVT_kwDOBiArKM4AsZ5a
Status:      PVTSSF_lADOBiArKM4AsZ5azgjVcYY
Priority:    PVTSSF_lADOBiArKM4AsZ5azgjVccc
Size:        PVTSSF_lADOBiArKM4AsZ5azgjVccg
```

### Status Options

| Status | ID | When to use |
|--------|-----|-------------|
| Backlog | `9fce08f0` | Not yet planned for near-term work |
| Up Next | `a808d070` | Planned for upcoming work |
| In Progress | `b0d90514` | Actively being worked on |
| In Review | `e4081d22` | PR is open AND CI is green, and the author is requesting review ("someone look at this"). Not yet merged |
| Merged | `708f0888` | PR merged, not yet deployed. **Not for deploy-on-merge repos** (see Ticket Flows) |
| On Testnet | `a9783abd` | Deployed to testnet, awaiting verification — **exchange stack only** |
| Live - Verify | `707ea972` | Deployed to production, needs verification |

### Priority Options

| Priority | ID | When to use |
|----------|-----|-------------|
| P0 | `74c3b5cd` | Blocks users from core functionality (e.g., can't trade) |
| P1 | `4906e13b` | Important bug or feature, should be addressed soon |
| P2 | `029afb3e` | Moderate priority, can wait for next sprint |
| P3 | `72b439ae` | Low priority, nice-to-have |

### Size Options

| Size | ID | Rough scope |
|------|-----|-------------|
| XS | `524959a9` | 1-2 files, < 1 hour |
| S | `91485d8a` | 2-5 files, half a day |
| M | `5e3ab7e2` | 5-10 files, 1-2 days |
| L | `d8e26db7` | 10+ files or cross-repo, 3-5 days |
| XL | `13dfb346` | Major feature or refactor, 1+ weeks |

### Checklist for every new issue

1. Create the issue (no `--label` for type/priority/size)
2. Set the native Issue Type via API (`gh api ... -f type="Bug"`)
3. Add to project board and set **Status**, **Priority**, and **Size**
4. Optionally add category labels (`infra`, `research`, `blocked`, `documentation`)

### Example: Add issue to board with fields

```powershell
$itemId = gh project item-add 1 --owner Shareland --url <issue-url> --format json | ConvertFrom-Json | Select-Object -ExpandProperty id
gh project item-edit --project-id PVT_kwDOBiArKM4AsZ5a --id $itemId --field-id PVTSSF_lADOBiArKM4AsZ5azgjVcYY --single-select-option-id <status-id>
gh project item-edit --project-id PVT_kwDOBiArKM4AsZ5a --id $itemId --field-id PVTSSF_lADOBiArKM4AsZ5azgjVccc --single-select-option-id <priority-id>
gh project item-edit --project-id PVT_kwDOBiArKM4AsZ5a --id $itemId --field-id PVTSSF_lADOBiArKM4AsZ5azgjVccg --single-select-option-id <size-id>
```

## Working on Issues

When implementing a fix or feature linked to an issue:

1. **Reference the issue** in your PR body using `Relates to #<number>` or `Part of #<number>`
2. **Do NOT use `Closes #XX` or `Fixes #XX`** — auto-close on merge is disabled at the repo level, but avoid these keywords anyway to keep intent clear. Issues are closed manually after the change is deployed and verified.
3. **Move the issue** to "In Progress" when you start working on it.
4. **Keep it in "In Progress"** while the PR is iterating (red CI, Bugbot suggestions, unaddressed review feedback). Do NOT move to "In Review" while CI is failing.
5. **Move to "In Review" yourself when the PR is open AND CI is green** — it is the signal that you want review ("someone look at this"). Review and merge are handled by the maintainer (the project lead or the reviewer they delegated); do not merge your own PR. If review comes back with feedback, move the card back to "In Progress" while you address it.
6. **Move to "Merged"** when the PR is merged, **unless the repo deploys on merge** — in `shareland-docs`, `shareland-mission-control` and `shareland-prototypes` the merge already put it live, so go straight to "Live - Verify". Later statuses follow the release process.

## Issue Lifecycle

**The exchange stack** is the full flow:

```
Backlog → Up Next → In Progress → In Review → Merged → On Testnet → Live - Verify → Closed
                         ↑                ↑
                   PR created &      PR open + CI green;
                   CI iteration      author requests review
```

- **In Progress** = actively being worked on, includes open PRs still iterating on CI or review feedback
- **In Review** = PR open, CI green, review requested; the maintainer (or their delegated reviewer) reviews and merges from here
- Issues are **only closed manually** after the fix/feature is verified on the target environment (testnet or mainnet). This ensures the board reflects what's actually live, not just what's been coded.

**The other two flows (see Ticket Flows):**

- **Deploy on merge** (docs, mission-control, prototypes): `In Progress → In Review → Live - Verify → Closed`. No Merged, no On Testnet.
- **Deploy after merge** (common, infra, tooling): `In Progress → In Review → Merged → Live - Verify → Closed`. No On Testnet.

**Board columns lag reality.** Tickets are often not updated as work moves, so a column may be stale or appear to have skipped a stage. Do not infer deployment state from the column alone — check the actual release, deploy, or live URL, then correct the column.

## PM Pass Rules

When auditing the board ("take a PM pass"), cross-reference statuses with open PRs:

- **Check the repo's flow first** (see Ticket Flows) so you know which columns are even valid for that item.
- **In Progress** means active work is happening, including open PRs iterating on feedback. Do not flag In Progress items for lacking a PR — that is the expected state.
- **In Review** means the author is requesting review: the PR must be open with green CI. Flag any In Review item with no open PR or failing CI.
- **Merged** requires the PR to be merged, and does not apply to the deploy-on-merge repos. If a PR was merged but the issue is still In Review, move it to Merged.
- **In a deploy-on-merge repo, anything on `main` is already live** — it belongs in Live - Verify, not Merged.
- Check recently merged PRs and ensure their linked issues are moved to Merged (or On Testnet / Live - Verify if already deployed).
- Remember that columns lag reality — verify against actual deploys before drawing conclusions.

## Listing and Reading Issues

- List open issues: `gh issue list --repo Shareland/<repo> --state open`
- Read an issue: `gh issue view <number> --repo Shareland/<repo>`
- List project items: `gh project item-list 1 --owner Shareland --format json`

## Triage Sessions

When the user describes multiple issues at once (e.g., "I found 3 bugs today..."), create all issues and report back with issue numbers and links.
